# Board Processor — local real-board testing (#588 Phases 4-6).
#
# Copy to `.env.local` (gitignored) and fill in YOUR OWN values. Nothing in
# here is read by the deployed workspace: `web/dev_server.py` loads it for
# local development only, and the values are written into the board credential
# store — which is deliberately NOT the provider-keys store, because provider
# keys are injected into every agent subprocess and a board token must never be.
#
#   cp charts/workspace/.env.local.example charts/workspace/.env.local
#   $EDITOR charts/workspace/.env.local
#   python3 charts/workspace/web/dev_server.py 6080
#
# Use THROWAWAY accounts. Testing the write path means really commenting on and
# really transitioning real tickets.

# ── GitHub ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A fine-grained personal access token scoped to ONE throwaway repo, with
# permission `Issues: Read and write` and nothing else.
#   https://github.com/settings/personal-access-tokens
# Seed the repo with ~6 issues: a couple open, one closed as *completed*, one
# closed as *not planned* (that pair proves the state_reason round-trip), one
# with no body/assignee/labels (null optionals), and one with a label.
KC_BOARD_GITHUB_TOKEN=
KC_BOARD_GITHUB_OWNER=
KC_BOARD_GITHUB_REPO=

# ── Jira Cloud ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A free Atlassian site and an API token:
#   https://id.atlassian.com/manage-profile/security/api-tokens
# Paste the RAW token. Jira Cloud authenticates with Basic base64(email:token),
# and the credential store composes that header itself — pasting a pre-encoded
# blob is a known footgun, because it is unverifiable by eye and a stray
# newline from a shell `base64` fails only at request time.
# Seed a project (e.g. SUP) with ~6 issues across To Do / In Progress / Done,
# including at least one in a status your connector does NOT map, to prove it
# passes through as `raw` rather than being coerced.
KC_BOARD_JIRA_SITE=
KC_BOARD_JIRA_EMAIL=
KC_BOARD_JIRA_TOKEN=
KC_BOARD_JIRA_PROJECT=

# ── Zendesk Support ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A free trial (https://www.zendesk.com/register/) gives you a subdomain and an
# agent seat, which is all this needs.
#
# NOT an API token. Zendesk has withdrawn API-token creation from the admin UI
# (a new account has no "Add API token" button) and retires existing tokens on
# 2027-04-30. Every integration guide still showing `email/token:APITOKEN` is
# out of date. Use an OAuth access token:
#
#   1. Admin Center → Apps and integrations → APIs → OAuth clients → add one.
#      Note the *unique identifier* and set a redirect URL (any URL you can read
#      the address bar on — http://localhost is fine; nothing has to listen).
#   2. cd charts/workspace
#      python3 boards/zendesk_oauth.py start <subdomain> <identifier> <redirect>
#   3. Open the URL it prints, approve, and pass the `code` from the address bar
#      back: python3 boards/zendesk_oauth.py finish <code>. It prints the token.
#
# Do NOT use the implicit grant (response_type=token) that the integration
# guides show. Zendesk's admin UI creates PUBLIC clients, and a public client is
# only permitted authorization-code + PKCE — the implicit URL just refuses, in a
# way that reads like a misconfigured client. Codes expire in ~a minute, so run
# steps 2 and 3 back to back.
#
# A client showing "Inactive" is not a problem: it means no token has been
# issued yet, and it flips on its own once one is.
#
# SUBDOMAIN is the host label only — for https://acme.zendesk.com it is `acme`.
# No email is needed: a bearer token identifies the user by itself.
# Seed ~5 tickets across new / open / pending / solved, at least one `urgent`,
# one in `open` specifically (otherwise IN_PROGRESS is never exercised), and
# leave one with no assignee (null optionals).
KC_BOARD_ZENDESK_SUBDOMAIN=
KC_BOARD_ZENDESK_TOKEN=

# ── Local runtime ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where the dev server keeps board state, so nothing writes to /home/dev on
# your machine. Relative paths resolve against charts/workspace/.
KC_LOCAL_HOME=.devdata

# Board runs press against the pod's task ceiling. Lower it locally so a
# runaway run cannot spawn a dozen tmux sessions on a laptop.
KC_MAX_TASKS=4
KC_BOARD_MAX_CONCURRENCY=3
